WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.600
Many data science concepts build on&nbsp;
previous work with relational databases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.600
Companies have been capturing&nbsp;
and analyzing data for decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.520
Even today, the relational database management&nbsp;
system is the cornerstone of enterprise data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.240
You need to understand the DBMS&nbsp;
terms on your data science projects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.200
One place you'll likely run into them is&nbsp;
when you're focused on data warehouses.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.280
An Enterprise Data Warehouse or EDW is&nbsp;
a special type of relational database&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:39.440
that focuses on analyzing data. Traditional&nbsp;
databases are optimized for online transaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:46.080
processing or OLTP. A data warehouse is&nbsp;
used for analytical processing or OLAP.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.720
Think of it this way. The typical database&nbsp;
is focused on working with data in real-time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.800
A data warehouse is focused on analyzing&nbsp;
what already happened. Imagine you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.000
a website that sells running shoes. You've&nbsp;
hired an engineer to create your database.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.080
They've created dozens of&nbsp;
different tables and relationships.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.400
There's a table for customer address, a table for&nbsp;
shoes, a table for shipping options and so on.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:21.040
Your web server would use a SQL statement to&nbsp;
search the data. When a customer buys a shoe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.720
it would join their shipping address to the shoe&nbsp;
then it would give them their shipping options.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.600
You'd want this database to be fast and&nbsp;
efficient. It's a transactional OLTP database.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.920
It should be optimized so that&nbsp;
when a customer finds their shoe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.000
joins it to their address and ships it to&nbsp;
their house, you want your database to focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.240
on speedy returns. Your customer&nbsp;
wants to do this in real-time.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.680
You may ask your database engineer to create&nbsp;
a script that uploads the data each day to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.800
your warehouse. Your data warehouse is&nbsp;
optimized for analytical processing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:02.320
It's an OLAP database focused on creating reports.&nbsp;
You might have a data analyst create a report to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:08.000
see if there's any connection between a customer's&nbsp;
address and the types of shoes they buy. You might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.080
find that people in warmer areas are more&nbsp;
likely to buy brightly colored shoes then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.960
you'd take this information and update the&nbsp;
way your website presents the data. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.840
change your website so that customers from warmer&nbsp;
climates see lighter shoes at the top of the page.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.840
Now, say that your website has been very&nbsp;
successful. It was bought by a company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.880
that sells sports clothing. That company&nbsp;
has one warehouse for all their websites.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.840
They want to take the data from your website&nbsp;
and combine it with all their other websites.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.640
The company will do something called&nbsp;
ETL, which stands for extract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.880
transform and load. They'll pull&nbsp;
the data from all their websites.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.360
Then they'll load the data into their Enterprise&nbsp;
Data Warehouse. The company will extract the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.680
from your website in a standard format then&nbsp;
they'll transform the data into something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.400
works well with their data warehouse. Their&nbsp;
warehouse could have a different schema than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:08.320
yours. The data analyst will spend most their time&nbsp;
scrubbing and joining the data so it will fit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:15.440
Finally, they'll load the transform data into the&nbsp;
data warehouse. A typical data science team will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:22.240
have similar challenges. They'll also very likely&nbsp;
use the same language. One thing you might hear is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:28.080
we need to ETL the data in the warehouse so we&nbsp;
can move it into the Hadoop cluster. What this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.960
means is that the data analyst need to transform&nbsp;
your data so they can move it into your cluster.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:39.040
Another area where this may come up is that many&nbsp;
organizations see Hadoop as a replacement for data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:45.440
warehouses. You see a lot of companies rewriting&nbsp;
their ETLs so they can load data into a Hadoop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:50.960
cluster then they faze out or shut down their&nbsp;
warehouse. Organizations that are hoping to save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:58.560
money by storing data on inexpensive hardware.&nbsp;
Data warehouses are usually a costly appliance. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.440
either case, you'll certainly run into these terms&nbsp;
and concepts as part of your data science team.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.560
Try not to get overwhelmed by the language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.200
Unfortunately, much of data science&nbsp;
is still about gathering the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:16.480
You'll spend many hours in ETL meetings&nbsp;
before you can ask any interesting questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:25.520
If you understand the terms and challenges then&nbsp;
you're more likely to get the data that you need.

